 "_note": "⚠️ NO LONGER A BLANKET LABEL. It was built as one -- every asset `antialiased`, only 8 inspected -- and on 2026-08-19 every disagreement between that label and the code was rendered to a 4x (then 6x) nearest-neighbour contact sheet and judged by eye. THE OPERATIONAL CRITERION, because 'looks blocky' is not one: IS THE PIXEL GRID THE ARTWORK? If nearest-neighbour is the right way to enlarge it, it is pixel_art; if the shapes are curves that merely have hard edges at this size, it is antialiased -- because --pixel-art switches the resize filter to nearest and would jag every curve. 9 assets were REAL pixel art the blanket pass got wrong: a lightning bolt, four flame sprites, a tiny PepeJedi, and star1/star2/star3, all with unmistakable staircase diagonals at 6x. 7 are genuinely undecidable at 32-128px and are `ambiguous` ON PURPOSE, which excludes them from every recall and specificity figure -- guessing them would move the score of the very thing that raised the question. ⚠️ THE FINDING THAT MATTERS MOST came from checking ORIGINALS rather than the disagreement list: the whole Blox-Fruits item family (yetifruit, shadowfruit, venomfruit, ... 16 assets) looks like chunky pixel art in its QUANTIZED twin and is smooth, soft-shaded 3D-style rendering in the original. Those stay `antialiased`. See references/lessons.md §32.",
